When a large grid is partitioned into equal tiles and reduced to a smaller output, compare each output cell against the full tile rather than testing only a favorite sample coordinate. Record the histogram and test mode, presence/absence, dominant non-background value, and positional candidates. Include ties and tiles whose most frequent value is the apparent background. Validate the candidate cellwise across the complete demo before applying it; request another example if unresolved tie or selection behavior could alter the test.

In task-197199f9 demo 1 (18x18 to 6x6, 3x3 tiles), every output value equals its tile's mode. This is a useful candidate, not yet a verified universal rule: one demo cannot establish tie-breaking or rule out another statistic that coincides on these tiles.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u022/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-evidence-processing/references/tile-statistics-analysis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/tiny-test-scaling.md", "content": "# Tiny-test scaling ambiguity

A uniform or very small test can be consistent with many transformations. Do not assume its eventual output will preserve the test dimensions or simply repeat the input. Derive output shape and tiling from demos, and separately identify the contents/orientation of each repeated tile. In particular, a demo can reveal a repeat factor without showing that the repeated tile is the original input: compare the tile cell by cell. If the rule's tile transform matters on the test but is underdetermined by current examples, request a demo that distinguishes plausible alternatives rather than treating an easy symmetric answer as proof of the general rule.

The task-5fb258d3 demo was 6×8→18×16, while the test contained two identical color-2 cells vertically. The submitted 6×2 all-2 answer was accepted, but this tiny uniform case did not establish a general rule for transforming the larger demo's nonuniform tile. Reuse only the conservative lesson: validate output dimensions and the transformation independently; do not extrapolate a broad repetition rule from this instance.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u022/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/arc-grid-reasoning/references/tiny-test-scaling.md"}

